PDS_VERSION_ID         = PDS3
RECORD_TYPE            = STREAM
OBJECT                 = MISSION
  MISSION_NAME         = "SELENE"
  OBJECT               = MISSION_INFORMATION
    MISSION_START_DATE = 2007-TBD
    MISSION_STOP_DATE  = "NULL"
    MISSION_ALIAS_NAME = "SLN"
    MISSION_DESC       = "

    Mission Overview
    ================

    The Selenological and Engineering Explorer (SELENE) will be launched by H-
    IIA rocket in 2007. Major objectives of the mission are to obtain scientific
    data for study about the origin and evolution of the moon, and to develop 
    technology for the lunar orbit insertion and spacecraft attitude and orbit 
    control. The scientific data will be also used for exploring the 
    possibilities of future utilization of the moon. 
    The SELENE spacecraft consists of main orbiter, relay-satellite, and VRAD 
    satellite. After lunar orbit insertion of the SELENE satellite two sub-
    satellites will be separately released into operational orbits for rely-
    satellite and VRAD satellite. After the release of subsatellites the main
    orbiter will reduce altitude to nominal circular orbit, following 12 months
    mission operation. 
    SELENE has a possibility of extending an optional mission after nominal 
    operation period. 

    Principal objective of SELENE project is to acquire observation data 
    globally in order to progress the "lunar science" for investigation of the 
    origin and evolution of the Moon, and to survey the possibility of 
    utilization of the Moon. 
    The project also possesses an objective in technological development and 
    accumulation of lunar orbit insertion and attitude control in lunar orbiting 
    in order to promote systematically and continuously lunar exploration.

    SELENE consists of a main orbiter and two sub satellites, Relay-satellite 
    (RSTAR) and VRAD-satellite (VSTAR). Main orbiter is composed with upper 
    module mounting science instruments and bus-system instruments except for 
    propulsion and battery systems, and lower module onboading propulsion and 
    battery systems.
    SELENE satellite assembled of the main orbiter, RSTAR and VSTAR will insert 
    into the trans-lunar orbit using an H-IIA rocket. After the SELENE 
    spacecraft enters into a circular lunar orbit, the Rstar and Vstar 
    separately insert into lunar ecliptic orbits, which acquire selenological 
    data such as gravity data for one year. Main orbiter observes the Moon using 
    scientific instruments from circular orbit of altitude 100 km for one year 
    of nominal period.
    JAXA tracking stations will be used primarily for tracking and operation for 
    initial cruising stage. Scientific data are received in JAXA UDSC (Usuda 
    Deep Space Center) and USC (Uchinoura Space Center) antennas. Satellite 
    operation and initial analysis of scientific data and bus data will be 
    performed in SELENE Operation and data Analysis Center (SOAC).

    Mission Phases
    ==============
 
    PRELAUNCH
    ---------
      Mission Phase Start Time  : ???
      Mission Phase Stop Time   : 2007-TBD

    LAUNCH
    ------
      Mission Phase Start Time  : 2007-TBD
      Mission Phase Stop Time   : 2007-TBD

    TRANS-LUNAR ORBIT INJECTION
    ---------------------------
      Mission Phase Start Time  : 2007-TBD
      Mission Phase Stop Time   : 2007-TBD

    LUNAR ORBIT INJECTION
    ---------------------
      Mission Phase Start Time  : 2007-TBD
      Mission Phase Stop Time   : 2007-TBD

    SCIENCE OBSERVATION
    -------------------
      Mission Phase Start Time  : 2007-TBD
      Mission Phase Stop Time   : 2008-TBD

    OPTIONAL MISSION
    ----------------
      Mission Phase Start Time  : 2008-TBD
      Mission Phase Stop Time   : TBD
"
  MISSION_OBJECTIVES_SUMMARY = "

    Mission objectives
    ==================
    SELENE mission has major objectives relevant with lunar science including
    investigation of future utilization, and engineering developments.

    Orbital science missions are issued as follows;
    - Global observation by X-ray spectrometer, gamma-ray spectrometer, lunar
      imager/spectrometer, lunar sounder and lunar magnetometer.
    - Measurement of lunar environment by charged particle spectrometer, plasma 
      instrument, lunar magnetometer, electromagnetic wave receiver onboard 
      lunar sounder equipment.
    - Differential VLBI measurement by radio sources.
      Plasma measurement of terrestrial ionosphere by upper atmosphere plasma 
      imager.
    - Detection of tenuous ionosphere by radio science.
    - Observation of planetary electromagnetic wave by passive wave receiver.
    - Gravity measurements of lunar far-side by four-way Doppler via relay 
      satellite.
    - High spatial resolution measurement by magnetometer, gravity, and gamma-
      ray spectrometer in optional extended mission period.
    - Broadcast of high definition images of the Earth and the Moon.

    The mission objectives in engineering development are as follows;
    - Successful injection of SELENE satellites into nominal lunar orbits.
    - Successful 3-axis stabilized attitude control and orbit correction 
      maneuver in orbiting the Moon.
    - Acquisition of navigation data for obstacle detection and high speed data 
      handling technologies in future landing mission.

    Instruments Summary
    ===================

    X-ray Spectrometer (XRS)
    ------------------------
    Investigation Description. 
    XRS sensors are developed to determine the surface chemical composition by
    measuring X-ray fluorescence of major elements radiated by the solar X-ray.
    Elemental abundances will provide us basic clue to understanding the lunar 
    origin of formation and evolution. Absolute elemental abundances of 90% 
    surface area for major elements of Al, Si, Mg, and Fe will be determined 
    within 10% uncertainty in the normal quiet sun. In high solar activity 
    secondary major elements such as Na, Ca, Ti are certainly excited over the 
    detection limits. 
    Spatial resolution of 20 km of lunar surface will be realized from altitude 
    of 100 km orbit. This requirement is estimated by minimum size of flat 
    floored type craters, which has possibility of resurfacing the rock type 
    different from surrounding crater ejecta. This size is a minimum scale of 
    geological structure necessary to determine the bulk composition of lunar 
    crust.

      Specification of XRS
      X-ray Fluorescence detector (XRF-A): X-ray detectable Charge Coupled 
      Device (CCD), ca.100 cm2 of effective detection area, spatial resolution 
      20 km @100 km altitude. 
      Solar X-ray monitor (SOL-BC): PIN Photo-diode and X-ray detectable CCD
      Electronics (XRS-E): detector control, data processing, data handling and 
      electric power supply.

    Gamma-ray Spectrometer and Charged Particle Spectrometer (GRS/CPS)
    ------------------------------------------------------------------
    Gamma-ray spectrometer and charged particle spectrometer commonly use data 
    handling electronics, which interfaces with Data Handling (DH) subsystem of 
    spacecraft.

      Gamma-Ray Spectrometer (GRS)
      ----------------------------
      Investigation Description
      GRS instrument will measure gamma-rays with 100 keV to 10 MeV. The primary 
      object is to provide global mapping data on the lunar surface abundance of
      a series key elements such as U, Th, K, O, Si, Mg, Fe, Ti, Ca, Al and H.
      The abundance of water ice in the permanently shaded craters at both lunar
      poles will clearly be determined by this mission. These measurements in 
      combination with mineralogical data from other instruments will 
      significantly improve our understanding of the composition and structure
      of the lunar crust, refractory element content of the bulk Moon, and the
      nature of impact processes. 

        Specification of GRS
        Gamma-ray Detector (GRD): N-type coaxial high-purity Germanium (HP-Ge) 
        crystal with ca. 250 cc volume, Cooling by Stirling mechanical 
        refrigerator to 90 K, 
        Anticoincidence shield of Bismuth Germanate (BGO) and plastic
        scintillater.
        Spatial resolution of 120 km @100 km altitude.
        Cooler Driving Unit (CDU): DC/AC power supply for Stirling refrigerator.
        Electronics (GPE): common use with CPS for sensor control, data-
        processing, 
        data handling, and electric power supply.

      Charged Particle Spectrometer (CPS)
      -----------------------------------
      Investigation Description
      The CPS will used to measure charged particle environment of the Moon; 
      alpha-particle from the lunar surface, and cosmic ray particle in circum-
      lunar interplanetary space. Alpha-particle indicates Rn-gas outflow from 
      lunar surface due to tectonic activity. Silicon detectors measure the
      kinds and energy range of cosmic ray particles such as electron, proton,
      He, O, and Fe varying with time and space.

        Specification of CPS
        Alpha-ray Detector (ARD): Silicon detectors of effective area of 300
        cm2.
        High energy Isotope Detector and Low energy Particle Detector (HID/LPD): 
        Silicone detectors for 0.3 to 1 MeV of electrons, 0.1 to 60 MeV of 
        protons, and 2.5 to 370 MeV/n of heavy ions.  

    Lunar Imager/ Spectrometer (LISM)
    ---------------------------------
    LISM consists of three sub-systems of Terrain Camera (TC), Multi-band Imager 
    (MI), and Spectral Profiler (SP) which share some parts of structure and 
    electronics. It will obtain topographic and compositional data of the Moon 
    surface which can be used for mapping of detailed geological units of the 
    Moon surface and identifying materials in each unit. The quality of the data 
    obtained by LISM will be much higher in spatial resolution, wavelength 
    resolution, and S/N than that by past lunar missions. The sensors are 
    controlled and acquired data are compressed by common data processing unit 
    (DPCU). Electric power are supplied to each subsystem by a commonly used 
    unit (PCDU).

      Multi-band Imager (MI)
      ----------------------
      Investigation Description
      MIis a high-resolution multi-band imaging camera consisting of two sub-
      systems; 
      MI-VIS for visible imagery and MI-NIR for near infrared imagery. Each
      system has a same type of nadir-directed telescope of 65 mm of focal
      length and a different two-dimension detector. MI takes push-broom imaging
      data by using five bands for MI-VIS and four bands for MI-NIR to identify
      types of soils and rocks. MIspatial resolution is 20 m for MI-VIS and 60 m
      for MI-NIR, which is adjusted for detailed investigation of interesting
      structures and material distributions on the Moon.

        Specification of MI
        Sensors: Si-CCD and InGaAs
        Optics/imaging: two reflecting telescopes/ push-broom type.
        FOV/Foot print: 11 degrees/ 19.3 km @100km altitude.
        Detectable wavelength range/band quantity/band width: 0.4-1.6um/ nine 
        bands/ 20-50nm.

      Spectral Profiler (SP)
      ----------------------
      Investigation Description
      SP is a visible to near-infrared line profiling spectrometer. Its primary 
      science goal is to map the rock type and mineral abundance/composition on 
      the lunar surface globally through reflectance spectroscopy in the 500 - 
      2600 nm region with unprecedented high spectral resolution and S/N.

        Specification of SP
        Sensors: one-dimensional photodiode array for visible range and InGaAs
        for near-infrared range 
        Optics/wavelength dispersion: Cassegrain-type foreoptics/Plane grating 
        Observational wavelength range: 0.5-2.6um
        Wavelength resolution: 6-8 nm

      Terrain Camera (TC)
      -------------------
      Investigation Description
      TC is a push-broom imaging camera with a spatial resolution of 10 m from
      100 km altitude. It has two slant telescopes for stereo imagingÅBThe 
      stereoscopic observation of TC will be carried out at the middle solar 
      elevation angle conditions. The single-eyed observation will be also
      planned at the low solar angle (<30ÅK) conditions for detailed
      investigation of surface features of the Moon. TC will expand the coverage
      to almost all of the Moon and that in the form of digital data. The global
      and detailed investigation of the topography and geographical features of
      the Moon will be achieved by TC data.

        Specification of TC
        Sensor: Si-CCD
        Optics/ Imaging: two reflecting telescopes/ push-broom type.
        Field Of View/Foot print: 20 degrees/ 35 km @100km altitude.

    Lunar Radar Sounder (LRS)
    ------------------------
    Investigation Description
    LRS will survey the subsurface structure of the Moon by analyzing 
    electromagnetic HF signals which are radiated from spacecraft and reflected 
    from lunar surface and subsurface. The sounder mode operation of LRS will 
    investigate the tectonics of the Moon.
    LRS also observes natural radio waves of Sun, terrestrial and planetary 
    bodies in the lunar orbit of far-side quiet environment using onboard wide-
    range and high sensitivity radio-wave receiver. 

      Specification of LRS
      Antenna: 2 pairs of 30 m tip-to-tip dipole antenna for Tx and Rx using 15m
      bi-stem antenna elements. 
      Electronics: for sounder operation, consumption electric power of 51 W, 
      frequency of 4 - 6 MHz FM (1 and 15 MHz optional), Tx pulse width of 200 
      usec, pulse repetition of 50 msec, sweep rate of 10 kHz/usec, output power 
      of 800 W, echo observation range of surface +5 km - -25 km, range 
      resolution of 75 m, receiver sensitivity of less than 0.3 uVrms. For 
      passive mode, electric power of 25 W, observation mode of Dynamic Spectrum
      (DS), Polarization Spectrum (PS) and Waveform Capture (WFC), frequency 
      range of 10 - 30 MHz

    Laser Altimeter (LALT)
    ----------------------
    Investigation Description
    LALT transmits laser pulses to nadir direction and measures the round trip 
    time to the surface of the moon to determine the topography of the Moon 
    globally including its polar regions. LALT builds a spherical harmonic 
    expansion model of the lunar topography with much higher order degrees than 
    that made previously by Clementine mission so on. LALT constructs a grid map 
    of topography, which provides the absolute reference to the DEM (Digital 
    Elevation Map) produced by TC(Terrain Camera).

      Specification of LALT
      Laser:  Nd:YAG; wavelength of 1064 nm, 100 mJ output, 1 Hz repetition 
      rate.
      Collimator: Galilei refractor type with 7.3 cm diameter.
      Telescope: Cassegrain type, focal length 300 mm with 11 cm diameter 1mrad 
      field of view.
      Detector: Si-APD, 10nm band pass filter.
      Along track spacing: 1.6 km
      Footprint: 30 m in diameter. 

    Magnetic Field and Plasma Experiment (MAP)
    ------------------------------------------
    Lunar Magnetometer (LMAG) and Plasma energy, Angle and Composition 
    Experiment (PACE) commonly use an electronics box (MAP-E) to interface with 
    the handling unit of bus subsystem.

      Lunar Magnetometer (LMAG)
      -------------------------
      Investigation Description
      LMAG will observe the lunar and its surrounding magnetic fields for 
      magnetization structure of the lunar magnetic anomalies, deformation of 
      magnetic fields by the Moon and solar wind interaction, and electric 
      conductivity structure of the lunar interior observing induced field.
      Three component fluxgate magnetometer will be mounted on the top of 12 m 
      extendable mast. The sensor alignment will be frequently monitored+/- due
      to short time repetition of day and night in orbiting the Moon.

        Specification of LMAG
        Sensor (MGF-S): Dynamic range of +/-32, +/-256, +/-1024 and +/-65536 nT; 
        accuracy of 0.5 nT.
        Mast (MST-S): 12 m length with sensor direction monitor.

      Plasma energy, Angle and Composition Experiment (PACE)
      ------------------------------------------------------
      Investigation Description
      PACE will carry out energy- and mass-analysis of electrons and ions in 
      circum lunar space in order to study Moon-Solar wind interaction, Moon- 
      Earth' magnetosphere interaction, sputtering process of surface
      constituent elements by solar wind, and lunar surface remnant magnetic
      field.  PACE consists of IMA (Ion Mass Analyzer) IEA (Ion Energy Analyzer)
      and two ESA (Electron Spectrum Analyzer) sensors. These sensors have an
      axial symmetric structure around rotation axis and keep a field of view 
      2*PI str using azimuth direction sweep of electrostatic polarizer. The
      whole direction observation is realized by pair of sensors onboard opposed
      spacecraft panels. A top hat electrostatic analyzer placing angular
      scanning deflectors will measure 3D analysis of electron energy. 
      IMA additionally equips an ion mass analyzer of LEF(Linear Electric 
      Field)/TOF (Time Of Flight) type.

        Specification of PACE
        Ion Mass Analyzer (IMA): Energy range of 5 eV/q - 28 keV/q, Mass range
        of 1 - 60, install on +Z panel.
        Ion Energy Analyzer(IEA): Energy range of 5 eV/q - 28 keV/q,install on
        -Z.
        Electron Spectrum Analyzer (ESA): Energy range of 5 eV/q - 17 keV/q, 
        install on +Z and -Z panels.
        Electronics (MAP-E): common use with LMAG for sensor control, data-
        processing, 
        data handling, and electric power supply.

    Upper-atmosphere and Plasma Imager (UPI)
    ----------------------------------------
    Investigation Description
    Lunar orbiter SELENE is an ideal platform for simultaneous aurora imaging of 
    Earth's both Polar Regions. The Earth from the lunar orbit is a disk of only 
    2 degrees, thus aurora imaging of both pole can be achieved simultaneously 
    with small distortion. Moon has no atmosphere, which leads no active 
    emission of light near the spacecraft, thus we will have a high quality 
    imaging. UPI will observe ionosphere to magnetosphere of the Earth in the 
    range of visible to ultraviolet light. 

      Specification of UPI
      Visible Imager (UPI-TVIS): Schmidt-CaÇìsegrain optics, FOV of 2.38 X 2.38 
      degrees and 0.0047 deg. resolution, CCD with Peltier cooler.
      Extreme Ultra-violet Imager (UPI-TEX): Mo/Si multi-layer coated mirror, 
      detector of MCP with 2D resistive anode, FOV of 10 X10 degrees with angle
      resolution of 0.08 degrees.
      Gimbals (UPI-G): view direction control installed on the X-panel.
      Electronics (UPI-E): sensor control, data-processing, data handling, and 
      electric power supply.

    Radio Science (RS)
    ------------------
    Investigation Description
    RS will try to confirm the existence of lunar ionosphere by the radio 
    occultation technique. RS receives the radio waves from VRAD satellite and 
    detect the small deviation of the frequency influenced by the lunar 
    ionosphere at lunar occultation of VRAD satellite. Non-modulated, coherent 
    signal of s/x frequencies transmitted from the VRAD satellite are received 
    by UDSC 64 m antenna.

    Four-way Doppler Measurements by Transponders onboard Relay Sub-satellite  
    (RSAT-1) and main orbiter (RSAT-2)
    --------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Investigation Description
    RSAT plans satellite to satellite Doppler measurement by using relay sub-
    satellite (Rstar) to the main orbiter behind the Moon. A complete coverage 
    of Doppler tracking from the orbiter at sufficient low altitude will 
    significantly improve lunar gravity model and will contribute for future 
    geophysical study of interior and tectonics on the Moon. RSAT is a 
    communication subsystem on the Rstar for 4-way Doppler tracking among the 
    main orbiter, the Rstar, and Usuda Deep Space Center as well as 2-way range 
    and Doppler measurements between the Rstar and UDSC. Two transponders, RSAT-
    1 and RSAT-2 are installed in the Rstar and the main orbiter. 

      Specification of RSAT
      Relay satellite transponder (RSAT-1): S-band receiver, transmitter, S-X 
      frequency, command decoder, telemetry encoder, S/X power amplifier, S-band 
      diplexer, power supply. 
      Antenna: S-band dipole antenna, S-band omni-directional antenna, X-band 
      dipole antenna.
      Transponder onboard main orbiter opposed to Rstar (RSAT-2): S-band 
      receiver and transmitter, S-band diplexer.

    VLBI Radio Source (VRAD-1, VRAD-2)
    ----------------------------------
    Investigation Description
    VRAD mission measures angular distances between two radio sources (VRAD-1 
    and VRAD-2)onboard Rstar and Vstar orbiting the Moon, and Quasars by
    differential VLBI. VRAD improves accuracy of selenodetic parameters 
    particularly spherical harmonics of the lunar gravitational field by one or 
    two orders in cooperation with 4-way Doppler measurements and 2-way Doppler 
    and ranging measurements between the main orbiter and the Rstar. A VLBI 
    ground network with 2000 km baseline can determine a phase delay with the 
    accuracy of 3.3 ps for using carrier wave of X-band (8.4GHz) with 0.17 rad. 
    (10 degrees) sensitivity, which is equivalent to the accuracy in angular 
    distance of 5.3 Å~ 10-10 (1.1 Å~ 10-4arcsec) or about 20 cm on the Moon.  

      Specification of VRAD-1 and VRAD-2
      Transmitter: 3 S-band carrier waves and X-band wave.
      Antenna: S-band dipole antenna, X-band dipole antenna
      VRAD-2 equips S-band receiver, diplexer, telemetry encoder and command 
      decoder.

    High Definition Television System (HDTV)
    ----------------------------------------
    Investigation Description
    HDTV will take pictures and movies of the Moon and the Earth such as "Earth 
    rise" by high definition television cameras. Clear and vivid pictures from 
    the space take advantages in promoting popular understandings of space and 
    space development by Japanese nation, furthermore, give dreams and interests 
    in science technology to young nation of next generation. 
    HDTV uses wide-angle and telescopic color cameras with fixed focal length.
       
      Specification of HDTV
      Detectors: 3 CCD chips with 1920 Å~ 1080 pixels.
      FOV: 50.2 Å~ 28.2 degrees for wide angle, 15.6 Å~ 8.8 degrees for 
      telescopic.
      Exposure: Sampling interval of 1/59.94 seconds using an electronic 
      shutter.
      Operation mode: Movie mode, still mode.
      Data compression: Flame compression (DCT scheme).
      Semiconductor memory: ca. 2 Gbyte (corresponds to ca. 1 minute movie 
      data).
      Maximum transfer rate: 7.6 Mbps (RS-422, CCSDS packeting scheme).

"
  END_OBJECT         = MISSION_INFORMATION

  OBJECT             = MISSION_HOST
    INSTRUMENT_HOST_ID = SLN

    OBJECT             = MISSION_TARGET
      TARGET_NAME      = MOON
    END_OBJECT         = MISSION_TARGET

  END_OBJECT           = MISSION_HOST
END_OBJECT           = MISSION

END
